# Method Overloading

Method overloading is a mechanism which allows us to define multiple methods with the same name but with different parameters.

We can define a method with the same name but different parameters.

The compiler will choose the best method based on the number of parameters and the type of parameters.

The following example shows a method with different parameters:

```java
public class MethodOverloadingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create an instance of the class
        MethodOverloadingDemo obj = new MethodOverloadingDemo();

        // Call the methods
        obj.add(10, 20);
        obj.add(10.5, 20.5);
        obj.add(10, 20, 30);
    }

    // The add() method with two integer parameters
    public void add(int a, int b) {
        System.out.println(a + b);
    }

    // The add() method with two double parameters
    public void add(double a, double b) {
        System.out.println(a + b);
    }

    // The add() method with three integer parameters
    public void add(int a, int b, int c) {
        System.out.println(a + b + c);
    }
}
```

The output of the above program is:

```
30
31.0
60
```

## Overloading in Java

The process of overloading a method is called method overloading. Two methods having the same name but different parameters are called overloaded methods. The method overloading is a mechanism to achieve the same functionality with different parameters.

In Java, we can overload a method when the method name is the same but the parameters are different.

In the above example, we have three methods with the same name but different parameters. The compiler will choose the best method based on the number of parameters and the type of parameters.

## Why Overloading?

Overloading in Java is used to achieve the same functionality with different parameters

